sociated [2, 9]. In particular, given any increasing functions $f,g:{2}^{E}\\to ℝ$ that depend on disjoint coordinates:\n\n | $${𝔼}_{\\mu }[f]\\cdot {𝔼}_{\\mu }[g]\\ge {𝔼}_{\\mu }[f\\cdot g]$$  | \n\nAn easy consequence is the following:\n\n###### Fact 10 (Fact 3.16 in [22]).\n\nFor any $\\lambda $-uniform spanning tree distribution $\\mu $, for any $S\\subseteq E$, any $k\\ge ℝ$, and any $e\\in E$ we have:\n\n  1. 1.\n\nIf $e\\notin S$ then ${\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\mid {S}_{T}\\ge k\\right]\\le {\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\right]$ and ${\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\mid {S}_{T}\\le k\\right]\\ge {\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\right]$.\n\n  2. 2.\n\nIf $e\\in S$, then ${\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\mid {S}_{T}\\ge k\\right]\\ge {\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\right]$ and ${\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\mid {S}_{T}\\le k\\right]\\le {\\mathbb{P}}_{\\mu }\\left[e\\in T\\right]$.\n\nThere is also a useful consequence of negative association when applied to a homogeneous distribution. (Recall that a polynomial is homogeneous when all terms have the same degree. A distribution is homogeneous when all outcomes have the same number of elements.)\n\n###### Fact 11 (Fact 3.17 in [22]).\n\nFor any $\\lambda $-uniform spanning tree distribution $\\mu $, for any set of edges $S\\subseteq E$ and any $e\\notin S$, we have:\n\n | $${𝔼}_{T\\sim \\mu }\\left[{S}_{T}\\right]\\le {𝔼}_{T\\sim \\mu }\\left[{S}_{T}\\mid e\\notin T\\right]\\le {𝔼}_{T\\sim \\mu }\\left[{S}_{T}\\right]+{x}_{e}$$  | \n\nand similarly,\n\n | $${𝔼}_{T\\sim \\mu }\\left[{S}_{T}\\right]-{x}_{e}\\le {𝔼}_{T\\sim \\mu }\\left[{S}_{T}\\mid e\\in T\\right]\\le {𝔼}_{T\\sim \\mu }\\left[{S}_{T}\\right]$$  | \n\nWhen we apply one of these two facts, we will often simply say we are using negative association.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEdges that crowd each other out · Matpic (https://www.matpic.com/essays/edges-that-crowd-each-other-out/)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The uniform spanning forest conjecture asks for negative correlation of edges in a uniformly random forest of any finite graph. ... The tree proof uses electricity, and there is no network whose resistances count forests. The strong Rayleigh theory that handles trees has been extended to some generalisations of spanning trees, and forests are not among them.\n## Still open: forests and connected subgraphs #\n\nThe uniform spanning forest conjecture asks for negative correlation of edges in a uniformly random forest of any finite graph. It is a special case, at a particular limit, of conjectures about the random-cluster model of statistical physics, where edges are kept with a probability weighted by the number of components, with weight $q < 1$q<1. The connected-subgraph conjecture is another limit of the same model. Both are supported by every computation and both are open. Partial results establish them for special classes of graphs, by arguments that use the class’s structure and do not extend.\n\nWhat would settle them is unclear. The tree proof uses electricity, and there is no network whose resistances count forests. The strong Rayleigh theory that handles trees has been extended to some generalisations of spanning trees, and forests are not among them. A counterexample is not ruled out either, and a search in larger graphs guided by the structure of the closest calls is a plausible way one might be found. That the closest calls approach one so steadily is the strongest hint that if the conjecture is true, it is true with no room to spare.\n\n## Electricity for one family, faith for the next #\n\nUniform random spanning trees repel their edges because contracting a resistor can only lower the resistance across another, which is Rayleigh’s principle. The balanced bridge is the one place where the repulsion vanishes. Forests and connected subgraphs, the trees’ nearest relatives, repel on every one of the hundreds of thousands of pairs that can be checked, ever more faintly as graphs grow, with no reason anyone can prove. Matchings, which look similar, attract edges two steps apart and fail at once. The difference between a theorem, a conjecture and a falsehood sits in how the constraint on a random edge set is shaped.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nnew rounding techniques for the design and analysis of (https://studylib.net/doc/7979129/new-rounding-techniques-for-the-design-and-analysis-of)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.5 years ago; Crawled: today;     In this thesis we are mainly interested in measures with negative association. ...     Feder and Mihail [FM92] proved that uniform measures on balanced matroids (and in particular ...     λ-random spanning tree distributions) are negative associated. ...     Proof.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProbability on Graphs - Statistical Laboratory (https://studyres.com/doc/4720348/probability-on-graphs---statistical-laboratory?page=223)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.4 years ago; Crawled: last month;     Uniform spanning tree ...     2.6 Review the proof of Theorem 2.11, as follows. ... Using the Feder–Mihail Theorem\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNegative dependence and the geometry of polynomials (https://geodesic.mathdoc.fr/articles/10.1090/S0894-0347-08-00618-8/)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Negative dependence and the geometry of polynomials ... This class covers important models such as determinantal measures (e.g. product measures and uniform random spanning tree measures) and distributions for symmetric exclusion processes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDatabase | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/data